BLOCK|h1|1|Cluster-lang: Chapter 5 — Concurrency & Channels
BLOCK|p||Cluster-lang supports high-performance concurrency through lightweight green threads and copyable thread-safe communication channels.
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|1. Spawning Green Threads (`spawn`)
BLOCK|p||Green threads (lightweight fibers) are spawned using the `spawn` keyword. They run asynchronously on the compiler's worker thread pool.
BLOCK|h3|3|Spawning a Function
BLOCK|code|python|fn calculate(id: int):
    put "Task started:"
    put id

fn main():
    spawn calculate(1)      // Run calculate(1) asynchronously
    spawn calculate(2)      // Run calculate(2) asynchronously
BLOCK|h3|3|Spawning a Block of Code
BLOCK|p||You can also spawn an inline block of code using `spawn:` statement:
BLOCK|code|python|fn main():
    spawn:
        put "Task runs in thread pool"
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|2. Channels (`Channel[T]`)
BLOCK|p||Channels allow safe, synchronized data communication between different green threads without race conditions.
BLOCK|h3|3|Declaring a Channel
BLOCK|p||Declare a channel by specifying the generic type parameters and capacity:
BLOCK|code|python|ch := Channel[int](10)      // Channel holding integers, capacity = 10
BLOCK|h3|3|Sending and Receiving Data
BLOCK|list||`ch.send(value)`: Sends data into the channel (blocks if the channel capacity is full).
BLOCK|list||`ch.recv()`: Receives data from the channel (blocks if no data is available).
BLOCK|code|python|fn worker(ch: Channel[int]):
    ch.send(42)            // Sends 42 into the channel

fn main():
    ch := Channel[int](5)
    spawn worker(ch)
    
    val := ch.recv()       // Blocks until data is received
    put val                // Prints 42
BLOCK|p||Channels are pass-by-value/copyable safe and reference-counted under the hood, making them safe to copy and pass to spawned lambdas.
BLOCK|hr||
